[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known: "one issue, one fix queued".
Its reports hold several problems, each with its own fix. The parser fix that is queued covers
only one of them. Each problem now has a ticket of its own; the table below counts them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Fixing the parser leaves the booking-v3 hint and the one-day moves open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

Counted with the desk's own view, as it was exported: every report merged into #4471, each id once
(the export has overlapping pages, so some reports appear twice), filed by tenant and channel the way
the cause table on #4471 files them. The one-day moves stay counted where the desk files them, under
`parser`, and get a row and a ticket of their own because their fix is different.

## The problems

### parser: dates read month first

The shared parser reads every date month first; all our tenants write the day first. Day and month
swap: "Booked for 2 December. The invite says 12 February."

<ProblemTenants ticket={4471} problem="parser" />

### hint: booking-v3 tells people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint says, and the form
reads it the tenant's way. The result looks like the parser bug, but the parser fix will not touch
it: the hint has to change.

<ProblemTenants ticket={4471} problem="hint" />

### one-day: bookings move by one day

These are not swaps. The booking lands one day early or late, or differently for attendees in
different places ("Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday."). That points at
time zones when the invite is built. The desk already read them this way (`desk-notes.json`) and files
them under the parser by tenant; the parser fix will not change them.

<ProblemTenants ticket={4471} problem="one-day" ids />

## Why the old page showed one issue

- Merging went by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). The same
  subject comes from every problem, so the merge hid them.
- The cause table on #4471 already found the booking-v3 reports and marked them "not this ticket — no
  ticket of its own". Nobody filed that ticket.
- The one-day moves were only a footnote under `parser`.
- Intake's tag guesses from the reporter's browser. The cause follows the tenant and the channel, so
  the tag counts (parser, hint) do not match the problem counts.

